  "query_preview": "..\n\nAnother period of high magnitude warmth by November standards will \nremain firmly entrenched over the next 24 hours. This pattern \ngoverned by increasing depth of southerly flow within the existing \nelevated upper level height field held immediately downstream of an \ninbound elongated upper trough now ejecting into the plains. Late \nafternoon observational trends show temperatures pushing well into \nthe 60s - or a solid 15 to 20 degrees above average and near record \nterritory for the day. Similar thermal profile still entrenched \nheading into Sunday, but with perhaps a bit more higher level \ncloudiness to place a slightly lower ceiling on prospective highs. \nTemperatures again peaking in the low to mid 60s to finish the \nweekend. While the better dynamics tied to the mid level trough \nremain north, impinging height falls will draw the attendant cold \nfront across lower Michigan late Sunday. Brief increase in quality \nand depth to pre-frontal moisture will maintain potential for some \nlight shower production from northwest to southeast from late \nafternoon through the evening hours. \n\nStretch of notably colder conditions set for the early week period. \nInitial period of cold air advection emerges starting Sunday night. \nThe advective process will be reinvigorated both Monday and Monday \nnight as two separate mid level features roll through and \neffectively deepening the mean trough with some increasing depth to \nnortherly flow of arctic origin. Seasonable conditions Monday, with \ntemperatures then trending below normal across the Tue-Wed window \nwith highs in the 30s. An uptick in coverage of light snow \nshowers/flurries plausible particularly north of I-69 Monday evening \ninto the overnight hours, as a focused area of greater forced ascent \nwith the trailing, more dynamic wave engages the ongoing moisture \nflux off northern lake Michigan. Outgoing forecast highlights lower \nend precipitation potential at this stage with further refinement \nlikely.   \n\nDeep layer stability held within prevailing low to mid level ridging \naffords dry and seasonably cool conditions Wednesday. Forecast turns \nless defined for the late week period, subject to behavior of a mid \nlevel wave projected to organize over the northern plains and a \nseparate feature ejecting across the southern conus. Mixed signal \nacross the ensemble solution space in terms of precipitation \npotential locally across this window, with dependence on system \ntrajectory and downstream pace. NBM precipitation chances held in \nthe chance range /30-40%/ remain appropriate for Thanksgiving. \nTemperatures remain generally seasonable during this time.",
